// A `type` declared INSIDE A FUNCTION BODY, shadowing a module-scope name of a different
// shape. RULED legal and lexically scoped on 2026-09-02 (DECISIONS.md §"A `type` declared
// in a function body is legal and lexically scoped"): the parser has always admitted it,
// the precedent is that a nested NAMED `function` in the same position runs today, and a
// declaration form that is legal in a body for functions and not for types is a rule
// nobody would write on purpose.
//
// BUILT 2026-09-02 (D1045): the parser mints a body-scoped declaration under a unique name
// (`P` → `P$b1`), spells every reference in its lexical extent at that name, and hoists the
// declaration into `progStmts` — the module merge's own answer to "two declarations, one
// spelling", one scope in. The probe stays as the standing witness that it keeps running.
// Should print 3.
type P = { x: i32 }
function f(): i32 {
  type P = { x: i32, y: i32 }
  const p: P = { x: 1, y: 2 }
  return p.x + p.y
}
print(f())
